1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,910
Click.

2
00:00:00,660 --> 00:00:00,910
Click.

3
00:00:00,660 --> 00:00:03,510
You’ve done it a thousand times without thinking.

4
00:00:03,260 --> 00:00:05,459
But inside that little click is one

5
00:00:05,209 --> 00:00:07,353
of the cleverest machines you own.

6
00:00:08,303 --> 00:00:10,673
This is the humble retractable ballpoint.

7
00:00:10,423 --> 00:00:12,303
A barrel, a button and a tip.

8
00:00:12,053 --> 00:00:14,943
Most of us never give it a second thought.

9
00:00:14,693 --> 00:00:16,149
But unscrew the barrel,

10
00:00:15,899 --> 00:00:17,878
and it comes apart into a handful

11
00:00:17,628 --> 00:00:19,345
of beautifully simple parts.

12
00:00:22,196 --> 00:00:23,411
Laid out in a line,

13
00:00:23,161 --> 00:00:24,630
there’s the push button,

14
00:00:24,380 --> 00:00:25,748
a little rotating cam,

15
00:00:25,498 --> 00:00:27,119
the spring, the ink refill,

16
00:00:26,869 --> 00:00:28,796
and the two halves of the barrel.

17
00:00:28,546 --> 00:00:29,607
Just a few parts,

18
00:00:29,357 --> 00:00:30,896
and not one of them wasted.

19
00:00:30,646 --> 00:00:32,781
Every one of them has exactly one

20
00:00:32,531 --> 00:00:33,352
job to do.

21
00:00:35,202 --> 00:00:36,632
The magic is in the cam.

22
00:00:36,382 --> 00:00:38,361
Inside the barrel run little ridges,

23
00:00:38,111 --> 00:00:40,329
with deep channels between them and short

24
00:00:40,079 --> 00:00:41,002
ledges on top.

25
00:00:40,752 --> 00:00:41,834
Press the button,

26
00:00:41,584 --> 00:00:43,449
and its angled teeth push the cam

27
00:00:43,199 --> 00:00:44,379
down, and twist it,

28
00:00:44,129 --> 00:00:45,162
just a fraction.

29
00:00:44,912 --> 00:00:47,242
Let go, and the cam lands on a ledge.

30
00:00:46,992 --> 00:00:48,207
The tip is held out,

31
00:00:47,957 --> 00:00:48,932
ready to write.

32
00:00:48,682 --> 00:00:51,014
Press again, and it turns a little further,

33
00:00:50,764 --> 00:00:52,272
slips into a deep channel,

34
00:00:52,022 --> 00:00:54,161
and the spring pulls the tip back home.

35
00:00:55,861 --> 00:00:57,821
So one button does two jobs.

36
00:00:57,571 --> 00:00:59,460
The spring is always pushing back,

37
00:00:59,210 --> 00:01:01,821
and the cam remembers where it’s allowed to rest.

38
00:01:01,571 --> 00:01:03,498
Squeezed a little with every click,

39
00:01:03,248 --> 00:01:05,367
the spring gives the button its springy

40
00:01:05,117 --> 00:01:07,092
feel, and it’s strong enough to pull

41
00:01:06,842 --> 00:01:09,008
the tip home the moment the cam lets go.

42
00:01:09,708 --> 00:01:12,048
The refill itself is wonderfully simple.

43
00:01:11,798 --> 00:01:13,073
A thin tube of ink,

44
00:01:12,823 --> 00:01:15,338
with the writing tip pressed onto the end.

45
00:01:15,088 --> 00:01:16,271
When it runs dry,

46
00:01:16,021 --> 00:01:17,149
you swap it out,

47
00:01:16,899 --> 00:01:18,849
and the rest of the pen carries

48
00:01:18,599 --> 00:01:20,056
on, click after click.

49
00:01:21,806 --> 00:01:22,946
Now, the tip.

50
00:01:22,696 --> 00:01:24,558
This is the clever part,

51
00:01:24,308 --> 00:01:26,706
patented by László Bíró in 1938.

52
00:01:26,456 --> 00:01:29,176
Bíró was a newspaper editor in Budapest.

53
00:01:28,926 --> 00:01:31,543
He noticed that newspaper ink dried quickly,

54
00:01:31,293 --> 00:01:33,049
but it was far too thick for

55
00:01:32,799 --> 00:01:33,856
a fountain pen.

56
00:01:33,606 --> 00:01:36,114
His answer was a tiny rolling ball.

57
00:01:36,114 --> 00:01:38,121
At the very end sits that ball,

58
00:01:37,871 --> 00:01:40,332
often just seven tenths of a millimetre

59
00:01:40,082 --> 00:01:42,713
across, usually tungsten carbide or steel,

60
00:01:42,463 --> 00:01:45,150
held in a socket just loose enough to spin.

61
00:01:45,850 --> 00:01:47,550
As you write, the ball rolls.

62
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:48,742
Its top dips into thick,

63
00:01:48,492 --> 00:01:50,779
oil-based ink, and its bottom lays a thin

64
00:01:50,529 --> 00:01:52,070
film of it onto the paper.

65
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:53,020
The ink is a paste,

66
00:01:52,770 --> 00:01:53,920
so it doesn’t run,

67
00:01:53,670 --> 00:01:56,570
and fatty acids in it keep the ball turning smoothly.

68
00:01:56,320 --> 00:01:58,448
Most ballpoints rely on gravity too,

69
00:01:58,198 --> 00:02:00,640
which is why they won’t write upside down.

70
00:02:00,390 --> 00:02:01,882
And when you stop writing,

71
00:02:01,632 --> 00:02:03,554
the ball sits snugly in its socket,

72
00:02:03,304 --> 00:02:05,322
helping keep the ink from drying out.

73
00:02:06,672 --> 00:02:08,182
Put it all back together.

74
00:02:07,932 --> 00:02:09,236
The button presses,

75
00:02:08,986 --> 00:02:10,013
the cam turns,

76
00:02:09,763 --> 00:02:11,067
the spring returns,

77
00:02:10,817 --> 00:02:12,122
and the ball rolls.

78
00:02:11,872 --> 00:02:13,456
A complete little machine,

79
00:02:13,206 --> 00:02:14,636
sitting in your pocket.

80
00:02:15,685 --> 00:02:17,815
If you enjoyed this, subscribe.

81
00:02:17,565 --> 00:02:20,675
Next time, we’ll open up another everyday machine.

